  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Goblet (Pseudoclitocybe cyathiformis) is a small, delicate fungus named for its distinctive cup or goblet-shaped fruiting body. This member of the Pseudoclitocybaceae family is commonly found throughout New England's forests and woodlands.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Goblet Shape",
      "description": "Distinctive funnel or cup-shaped cap that deepens with age, resembling a small goblet or wine glass",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Decurrent Gills",
      "description": "Thin, crowded gills that run down the stem and are often waxy in appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and Color",
      "description": "Small fruiting body (1-3 cm wide) with a grayish-brown to tan cap and a slender, fragile stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Pseudoclitocybe cyathiformis grows on decaying wood, leaf litter, and mossy areas in deciduous and mixed forests throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its elegant goblet shape, this fungus is so small and fragile that it's easily overlooked by casual mushroom hunters, making it a favorite discovery for attentive mycologists.",
